    "created_at": "2026-11-06T04:19:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-06T04:19:21Z",
    "body": "Thanks for the proposal, I really like it. I also believe the AI agent use case is an important one: agents keep opening pages, and splits would keep the conversation visible beside them instead of losing it behind tab switches.\n\nI've been testing the prototype, and while looking at the _Existing splits_ open question I observed a behavior that seems worth checking. The COOP behavior itself is expected on its own; it just has a direct impact on how pane reuse would have to work.\n\n---\n\n**Environment:** Chrome for Testing 159.0.8138.0 (Chromium canary), `--enable-features=WindowOpenSplitTab`\n\n**Steps to reproduce:**\n\n1. From a page, open a split pane with a target name so it can be reused later:\n\n       window.open(\"https://www.youtube.com/\", \"myPane\", \"popup=0,split,splitposition=right,splitsize=50\")\n\n2. Let the document load. `youtube.com`, `x.com` and `instagram.com` all respond with `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups` (`tiktok.com`, `twitch.tv` and `vimeo.com` do not).\n3. Open another URL into the same pane: `window.open(otherUrl, \"myPane\")`, or an anchor with `target=\"myPane\"`.\n\n**Expected:** the pane is found by name and navigated (named targets take precedence, as today).\n\n**Actual:** after the COOP document loads, the pane's target name is cleared, and the handle from step 1 now reports `closed: true` (setting `location.href` on it does nothing). The step 3 lookup misses, so a new view opens. And since the window is already split, it's an ordinary tab.\n\n**Notes:**\n\n- Same steps without COOP (`tiktok.com`, `twitch.tv`): the name and the handle survive cross-origin navigations, and step 3 replaces the pane in place.\n- Same steps with `target=\"_blank\"`: no reuse at all, every open after the first is an ordinary tab fallback.\n- A local server sending `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin` gives the same result, so this is not specific to the allow-popups value.\n\n---\n\nSo pane reuse with existing mechanics depends entirely on the opened site's COOP policy.\n\n*Questions:*\nWould it make sense for panes opened via `split` to stay replaceable, or to define a replace mechanism?\nAnd should the explainer mention this interaction?\n\nHappy to share more feedback if that helps 🚀 ",
